\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Topic shortcut confound.}
One concern is whether a topic-level shortcut (jailbreak conversations discuss different topics than benign ones) could explain the detection results. Three observations argue against this. First, topic classification on the same jailbreak training data yields unstable OOD generalization (F1\,=\,0.541$\pm$0.207 on DD), with one seed falling below vanilla (0.252 vs.\ 0.312); if a topic shortcut drove detection, topic-supervised models should outperform, not underperform, unsupervised alternatives. Second, scrambled labels achieve detection equivalent to correct labels (F1 gap $\leq$3.9\,pp across all OOD evaluations), indicating that specific label content does not drive detection. Third, MLM without any classification labels achieves strong detection (F1\,$\geq$\,0.97 at $K \leq 5$ on DD), confirming that the benefit comes from domain exposure rather than any classification-based shortcut.

\paragraph{Single-LLM confound.}
Both training and synthetic OOD data are generated by Qwen3-8B, raising the concern that the evidence hierarchy reflects within-LLM stylistic transfer rather than beyond-lexical generalization. Evaluation on ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat} (204 human-written jailbreaks from LMSYS) provides partial resolution: all domain-adapted variants outperform vanilla by 18--42\,pp (Table~\ref{tab:human_data}), confirming that DAPT on synthetic text transfers to human-written attacks. ToxicChat is single-turn, so the relative ordering among domain-adapted variants reflects single-turn modality rather than variant quality. This evidence validates the core DAPT benefit but does not test the full multi-turn pipeline; multi-turn human jailbreak datasets are needed to confirm the complete hierarchy.

\paragraph{Variance reversal on human data.}
On synthetic OOD evaluations, classification supervision reduces cross-seed variance 4--20$\times$ relative to MLM. On ToxicChat (Table~\ref{tab:human_data}), this pattern reverses: 9-class exhibits high variance (std\,=\,0.087) while JB-MLM exhibits the lowest among domain-adapted variants (std\,=\,0.013). Classification supervision optimizes representations for the training distribution's structure, stabilizing predictions within the synthetic data manifold; when the test distribution shifts to human-written text, these distribution-specific representations become a source of instability. MLM's unsupervised objective imposes no distribution-specific classification boundaries, producing representations that generalize more uniformly across the style gap. Classification supervision and MLM thus serve complementary roles: the former stabilizes within the training distribution, the latter generalizes across distributions.

\paragraph{Encoder capacity confound.}
The cross-encoder comparison uses DeBERTa-v3-base (184M parameters) and RoBERTa-base (125M parameters), which differ in both pretraining objective and model capacity. We cannot fully disentangle whether DeBERTa-v3's greater DAPT needs reflect its RTD pretraining or its larger capacity. Size-matched comparisons (e.g., DeBERTa-v3-base vs.\ RoBERTa-large) would isolate the pretraining objective effect and are left for future work.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Our controlled ablation across two encoder architectures on synthetic multi-turn data spanning three OOD attack families identifies three findings. First, domain exposure via DAPT is the primary mechanism, bridging $\sim$60\,pp of the OOD gap on DeBERTa-v3 without any labels. Second, encoder pretraining method determines the difficulty threshold at which adaptation becomes critical: RoBERTa tolerates structurally simple attacks but fails on multi-persona ActorAttack (F1 0.981 $\to$ 0.466), though capacity differences (184M vs.\ 125M) cannot be fully disentangled from pretraining method. Third, classification supervision stabilizes predictions within the synthetic data distribution (variance reduced 4--20$\times$ vs.\ MLM-only on synthetic OOD), regardless of label correctness; however, this benefit does not transfer to human-authored data, where all classification variants underperform pure MLM (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}). DAPT's detection advantage persists under adversarial paraphrasing (McNemar $p < 0.001$), though absolute perturbation robustness is attack-family-dependent. Validation on human-written ToxicChat jailbreaks confirms that the domain-adapted vs.\ vanilla gap transfers beyond synthetic data; multi-turn generalization across generation sources remains to be verified.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Dataset scale and diversity.}
The training set contains 500 synthetic conversations generated by a single model (Qwen3-8B), and all multi-turn OOD evaluation data is also Qwen3-8B-generated. This scale limits statistical power: the perturbation evaluation ($n$=76) means tier boundaries in Table~\ref{tab:perturbation} correspond to 1--3 misclassifications. While ToxicChat (human-authored, single-turn) confirms that DAPT's detection advantage transfers across data sources (+18--42\,pp over vanilla; Section~\ref{sec:human_data}), multi-turn generalization across LLM generation sources remains to be verified. Whether the full evidence hierarchy, including classification supervision's within-distribution stabilization, generalizes to human-generated multi-turn attacks is open.

\paragraph{Lexical separability.}
TF-IDF logistic regression achieves F1 = 0.961 on OOD Deceptive Delight with near-perfect per-prefix performance, indicating substantial lexical separability on this attack family. On DD alone, we cannot distinguish whether continued pretraining captures beyond-lexical features or exploits the same lexical cues. ActorAttack resolves this question: TF-IDF collapses to F1 = 0.261 while domain-adapted models maintain F1 $\geq$ 0.89, a $\geq$63\,pp gap, confirming that continued pretraining captures features beyond lexical overlap on structurally complex attacks. Whether this beyond-lexical advantage extends to other structurally complex attack families beyond ActorAttack requires further evaluation.

\paragraph{Strategy annotation quality.}
Persuasion strategy labels are produced by Qwen3-8B with structured prompting. Inter-annotator agreement with a human sample ($n$=50) yields Cohen's $\kappa$=0.78, which indicates substantial but imperfect agreement. The small human validation sample and reliance on a single annotator model limit confidence in label quality. We plan to release the annotation prompts and data to enable independent verification.

\paragraph{OOD strategy prediction.}
While the 9-class model achieves high detection F1 on OOD attacks (0.997$\pm$0.005 on Deceptive Delight), its strategy-level predictions do not generalize: OOD strategy accuracy is 30.7\% ($\kappa$=0.126). The interpretability provided by the persuasion taxonomy is currently limited to known attack families where strategy labels are reliable.

\paragraph{Single-seed variants.}
Mean pooling results report a single-seed (seed 42) evaluation. Multi-seed validation is needed to confirm this finding.

\paragraph{FITD discriminative power.}
FITD provides minimal discriminative signal: both vanilla and 9-class achieve F1 $\geq$ 0.990 at all prefixes. FITD serves primarily as a confirmation that simple OOD attacks do not challenge any variant, rather than as a meaningful evaluation axis.

\paragraph{Scope.}
Only persuasion-mediated multi-turn attacks fall within scope. Single-turn token-level attacks~\citep{zou2023gcg}, non-persuasive multi-turn strategies, and attacks targeting modalities other than text are not addressed. The user-only input assumption (excluding assistant responses) may miss information in attacks where the model's own responses contribute to the escalation trajectory.

\paragraph{Computation cost.}
Stage~1 continued pretraining requires approximately 30 minutes on a single A100 GPU per variant. Stage~2 training completes in under 5 minutes. Total pipeline cost for all seven variants is under 4 GPU-hours, making the approach accessible for replication.

% method.tex — D024 compressed

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

DisPeD is a two-stage framework for multi-turn jailbreak detection. Stage~1 performs domain-adaptive continued pretraining (DAPT) on jailbreak conversation text to adapt a turn-level encoder. Stage~2 aggregates turn representations for conversation-level binary classification. To systematically identify what enables cross-attack generalization, we design seven Stage-1 variants that decompose the contributions of domain exposure, classification supervision, label quality, label granularity, and domain specificity. Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} illustrates the pipeline.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\fbox{\parbox{0.9\columnwidth}{\centering\vspace{2em}\textit{Architecture diagram placeholder}\vspace{2em}}}
\caption{DisPeD architecture. Stage~1 adapts a DeBERTa encoder on jailbreak conversation text (seven variant objectives). Stage~2 aggregates frozen \texttt{[CLS]} embeddings via BiGRU for conversation-level jailbreak detection. Only user turns are processed.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}

A conversation $C = \{u_1, a_1, \ldots, u_N, a_N\}$ alternates user turns $u_i$ and assistant responses $a_i$. Given a prefix of $K$ user turns, the model predicts $y \in \{0, 1\}$ (jailbreak vs.\ benign). We restrict input to user turns because adversarial intent resides in the attacker's messages. This formulation supports early detection from partial prefixes.

\subsection{Stage 1: Turn-Level Encoder Adaptation}
\label{sec:stage1}

Each user turn $u_i$ is encoded by DeBERTa-v3-base~\citep{he2023debertav3}; the \texttt{[CLS]} embedding $h_i \in \mathbb{R}^d$ serves as the turn representation. Seven variants, summarized in Table~\ref{tab:variants}, share the same training data but differ in objective.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Stage-1 encoder variants. All operate on the same training conversations except Wikipedia MLM (matched token count on Wikipedia text).}
\label{tab:variants}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{1.6cm}p{2.2cm}p{2.8cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Variant} & \textbf{Objective} & \textbf{Tests whether\ldots} \\
\midrule
9-class & 9-class persuasion + binary intent & full model \\
Binary & binary persuasion + binary intent & granularity matters \\
Scrambled & random 9-class + binary intent & label correctness matters \\
MLM-only & 15\% token masking & supervision is necessary \\
Topic & 5-class topic classif. & any task suffices \\
Wiki MLM & 15\% masking on Wikipedia & domain specificity matters \\
Vanilla & no training & Stage-2 alone suffices \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The \textbf{9-class} variant uses two classification heads: 9-class persuasion strategy classification ($L_{\text{strategy}}$) and binary jailbreak intent ($L_{\text{intent}}$), with combined loss $L = L_{\text{strategy}} + \alpha \cdot L_{\text{intent}}$ ($\alpha = 0.3$, selected from $\{0, 0.3, 0.5, 2.0\}$; all values yield F1\,=\,1.0 on IID). The taxonomy comprises eight persuasion strategies (rapport building, authority appeal, emotional manipulation, logical reframing, role assignment, gradual escalation, obfuscation, direct request) plus a none category, grounded in PAP~\citep{zeng2024pap} and Cialdini's principles~\citep{cialdini2001influence}. Strategy annotations are produced by Qwen3-8B with structured prompting that includes definitions and examples for each category; inter-annotator agreement between model annotations and a human sample ($n$=50) yields Cohen's $\kappa$=0.78.

The remaining classification variants test specific ablation hypotheses.
The \textbf{binary} variant collapses the 9-class taxonomy into persuasive vs.\ non-persuasive, testing whether label granularity matters for detection or robustness.
The \textbf{scrambled} variant assigns random permutations of the 9-class labels to turns, preserving the classification structure (cross-entropy loss, 9-class output space) while destroying label semantics; this isolates whether the classification objective itself provides representational benefits independent of label correctness.
The \textbf{MLM-only} variant applies 15\% masked language modeling on the same jailbreak conversation text without any classification head, testing whether unsupervised domain exposure suffices.
The \textbf{topic} variant trains a 5-class topic classifier (technology, health, finance, education, general) on the same conversations, testing whether \emph{any} classification task provides the same benefit or whether task alignment matters.
The \textbf{Wikipedia MLM} variant applies the same MLM objective to a token-matched sample of Wikipedia text, isolating the effect of domain-specific vs.\ general continued pretraining.
The \textbf{vanilla} baseline uses the pretrained DeBERTa-v3-base weights without any continued pretraining, measuring what Stage~2 alone can achieve.

\subsection{Stage 2: Conversation-Level Classification}
\label{sec:stage2}

Turn embeddings $\{h_1, \ldots, h_K\}$ from the frozen Stage-1 encoder are aggregated by a bidirectional GRU ($d_{\text{hidden}}$=128): $\bar{h} = [\overrightarrow{h_K};\; \overleftarrow{h_1}] \in \mathbb{R}^{256}$.
A two-layer MLP ($256 \to 64 \to 2$, ReLU activation, dropout 0.3) maps $\bar{h}$ to $\hat{y} \in \{0, 1\}$.
The Stage-1 encoder is frozen during Stage~2 training; only the BiGRU and MLP parameters are updated.
This frozen-encoder design ensures ablation fairness: all seven Stage-1 variants are evaluated under identical downstream conditions, isolating the effect of Stage-1 representation quality from Stage-2 classifier capacity.
A single-seed comparison suggests that mean pooling approaches BiGRU performance (Section~\ref{sec:decomposition}), though multi-seed validation is needed.

